2026-05-23 18:13:24,701 - INFO - thoughts: ["Here are three possible next steps for generating features to improve predictions for the KC1 software defect prediction task.\n\n### Step 1: Introducing ratios and normalized metrics\n```python\n# code_density\n# The ratio of McCabe's line count of code ('loc') to Halstead's total operators + operands ('n'). \n# This feature normalizes 'loc' by the number of operators and operands to understand the density of code lines relative to the complexity of the code.\n# Usefulness: Provides insights into code density. Very high or low densities may correlate with defects due to overly sparse or dense code.\ndf['code_density'] = df['loc'] / (df['n'] + 1e-5)\n```end\n\n```python\n# cyclomatic_to_volume\n# The ratio of 'v(g)' (cyclomatic complexity) to 'v' (Halstead's volume).\n# This feature captures the relative logical complexity of the code compared to its calculated volume.\n# Usefulness: Helps explain if modules are logically complex relative to their size, which can be a predictor of harder-to-maintain, defect-prone code.\ndf['cyclomatic_to_volume'] = df['v(g)'] / (df['v'] + 1e-5)\n```end\n\n```python\n# halstead_effort_per_loc\n# The ratio of Halstead's effort ('e') to McCabe's line count of code ('loc').\n# Usefulness: Normalizing Halstead effort by program size gives a measure of the effort required to implement the code relative to its size. Higher effort per line may signal complexity or defect liability.\ndf['halstead_effort_per_loc'] = df['e'] / (df['loc'] + 1e-5)\n```end\n\n---\n\n### Step 2: Encoding interactions between key variables\n```python\n# branch_loc_ratio\n# The ratio of 'branchCount' to 'loc', indicating the frequency of branching per line of code.\n# Usefulness: Modules with many decision points per line of code are often more prone to defects, since branching increases complexity.\ndf['branch_loc_ratio'] = df['branchCount'] / (df['loc'] + 1e-5)\n```end\n\n```python\n# unique_operator_to_operands_ratio\n# The ratio of unique operators ('uniq_Op') to unique operands ('uniq_Opnd').\n# Usefulness: A higher ratio indicates that operators dominate over operands, often corresponding to very formulaic or abstract code. This could highlight modules with unusual or error-prone coding styles.\ndf['unique_operator_to_operands_ratio'] = df['uniq_Op'] / (df['uniq_Opnd'] + 1e-5)\n```end\n\n```python\n# loc_with_comments_ratio\n# The ratio of lines of code with comments ('lOCode') to total code lines ('loc').\n# Usefulness: Code with sufficient documentation can often be less defect-prone as it is easier to review and maintain.\ndf['loc_with_comments_ratio'] = df['lOCode'] / (df['loc'] + 1e-5)\n```end\n\n---\n\n### Step 3: Extract statistical features using transformations\n```python\n# log_halstead_effort\n# The logarithm of Halstead's effort ('e').\n# Usefulness: Log transformation reduces skewness in the data, helping models better utilize this feature. 'e' has a wide range (0 to 234,743.54), which could dominate other features otherwise.\ndf['log_halstead_effort'] = np.log1p(df['e'])\n```end\n\n```python\n# sqrt_cyclomatic_complexity\n# The square root of cyclomatic complexity ('v(g)').\n# Usefulness: Square root transformation can reduce variability in values and help manage outliers better in metrics that are highly skewed, such as cyclomatic complexity.\ndf['sqrt_cyclomatic_complexity'] = np.sqrt(df['v(g)'])\n```end\n\n```python\n# zscore_essential_complexity\n# Z-score normalization of 'ev(g)' (essential complexity).\n# Usefulness: Helps standardize 'ev(g)' feature for better interpretability across models that thrive on normalized data (e.g., linear models). This ensures the feature's contribution is not dominated by other features of larger magnitude.\ndf['zscore_essential_complexity'] = (df['ev(g)'] - df['ev(g)'].mean()) / df['ev(g)'].std()\n```end\n\n---\n\n### Columns to drop (optional cleanup steps)\n```python\n# Total operators ('total_Op') and total operands ('total_Opnd') are redundant since their aggregate (total 'n') is already included in the data.\n# Explanation: Dropping these reduces multicollinearity while still retaining useful information in 'n'.\ndf.drop(columns=['total_Op', 'total_Opnd'], inplace=True)\n```end\n\n```python\n# locCodeAndComment\n# This column has very low variability (Median = 0.0, Q3 = 0.0) and doesn't add meaningful information or predictive power.\ndf.drop(columns=['locCodeAndComment'], inplace=True)\n```end\n\n```python\n# lOComment and lOBlank\n# These columns mostly contain zeros (Median = 0.0, Q3 = 0.0), providing little to no insight for the defect prediction task.\ndf.drop(columns=['lOComment', 'lOBlank'], inplace=True)\n```end", "```python\n# Step 1: Interaction Features for Code Complexity and Lines of Code\n# Feature name: loc_vg_ratio\n# Feature description: Ratio of lines of code (loc) to McCabe's cyclomatic complexity (v(g)). This indicates how complex the code is relative to its size.\n# Usefulness: A high ratio might indicate that the codebase is extensive but relatively less complex, whereas a low ratio suggests the opposite. This could be a useful signal for predicting defects, as overly complex code or highly dense logic can result in more defects.\ndf['loc_vg_ratio'] = df['loc'] / (df['v(g)'] + 1e-5)\n\n# Feature name: vg_branch_ratio\n# Feature description: Ratio of cyclomatic complexity (v(g)) to branchCount. Indicates the branching density relative to the complexity of the module.\n# Usefulness: Indicates how branching decisions are distributed across code complexity. High ratios might highlight problematic subroutines with excessive branching, which can be error-prone.\ndf['vg_branch_ratio'] = df['v(g)'] / (df['branchCount'] + 1e-5)\n\n# Feature name: essential_relative_complexity\n# Feature description: Ratio of McCabe's essential complexity (ev(g)) to cyclomatic complexity (v(g)). This measures the proportion of code complexity that is essential and cannot be reduced further.\n# Usefulness: Modules with a high proportion of essential complexity relative to total complexity may be more defect-prone, as they might have a more convoluted and less modular design.\ndf['essential_relative_complexity'] = df['ev(g)'] / (df['v(g)'] + 1e-5)\n```end\n\n```python\n# Step 2: Aggregated Halstead Metrics\n# Feature name: halstead_effort_density\n# Feature description: Density of Halstead effort (e) relative to the number of lines of code (loc).\n# Usefulness: Indicates the computational or cognitive effort required per line of code. High effort per line might point to modules that are overly complex and harder to debug, which could increase defect rates.\ndf['halstead_effort_density'] = df['e'] / (df['loc'] + 1e-5)\n\n# Feature name: halstead_volume_density\n# Feature description: Density of Halstead volume metric (v) relative to the number of lines of code (loc).\n# Usefulness: Captures the density of information or operations in the code. A high volume relative to lines of code might indicate compact but potentially difficult-to-maintain code.\ndf['halstead_volume_density'] = df['v'] / (df['loc'] + 1e-5)\n\n# Feature name: intelligence_to_difficulty_ratio\n# Feature description: Ratio of Halstead's intelligence metric (i) to difficulty (d).\n# Usefulness: Indicates the balance between the code's maintainability (intelligence) and difficulty. A lower ratio may suggest a higher defect risk due to code being harder to understand despite its intelligence score.\ndf['intelligence_to_difficulty_ratio'] = df['i'] / (df['d'] + 1e-5)\n```end\n\n```python\n# Step 3: Historical and Statistical Feature Transformations\n# Feature name: zscore_loc\n# Feature description: Z-score normalization of lines of code (loc) to standardize it relative to its distribution across modules.\n# Usefulness: Standardizes the loc feature for better comparability and better behavior across different models (especially distance-based ones like KNN or some regularization techniques).\ndf['zscore_loc'] = (df['loc'] - df['loc'].mean()) / df['loc'].std()\n\n# Feature name: log_effort\n# Feature description: Log transformation of Halstead effort (e) to handle the heavy-tailed distribution and large variance.\n# Usefulness: Reduces the skewness of the Halstead effort feature, ensuring downstream models don't disproportionately weight outliers.\ndf['log_effort'] = np.log(df['e'] + 1e-5)\n\n# Feature name: cumulative_cyclomatic_complexity\n# Feature description: Cumulative sum of cyclomatic complexity (v(g)) across modules to introduce historical context.\n# Usefulness: Provides context on the accumulated complexity of modules processed before the current one, which might affect defect occurrence in sequential processing scenarios, especially in larger systems.\ndf['cumulative_cyclomatic_complexity'] = df['v(g)'].cumsum()\n```end\n\n```python\n# Dropping Unnecessary Columns\n# Explanation why the column locCodeAndComment is dropped\n# This column is almost always zero (Median = Q3 = 0, Mean = 0.1233), indicating irrelevant variation or insufficient contribution to the task. It can be safely removed to reduce noise.\ndf.drop(columns=['locCodeAndComment'], inplace=True)\n\n# Explanation why the column lOComment is dropped\n# The count of lines of comments (lOComment) has a low mean (~0.9984) and median (0). This suggests it holds mostly zero values and has limited variability to aid the task.\ndf.drop(columns=['lOComment'], inplace=True)\n\n# Explanation why the column loc is not dropped\n# Despite potential correlation with other features, loc serves as a fundamental metric for code size. Retaining this could aid interpretability and feature interactions.\n# (No action taken in this case since loc is valuable.)\n```end"]
